<section xml:id="programming-languages_the-little-computer">
  <title>The Little Computer</title>

  <p>
    The Little Computer (sometimes called the Little Man Computer) is designed to explore how
    machine-level languages work.
    At a low level, all computers do is move around pieces of data and do basic math and logic
    operations on that data.
  </p>

  <p> The Little Computer has memory for 100 pieces of information and a simple CPU. The CPU has
    three <xref ref="computer-architecture-instruction-register"
          text="custom">
      registers</xref>: a program counter, an instruction register, and an accumulator. The CPU
    supports a very basic set of operations: it can get input and output, it can store data to
    memory and load it from memory, it can do addition and subtraction, and it can branch (or jump)
    to a different instruction if certain conditions are met. </p>

  <p> Rather than use binary to encode instructions, it uses decimal numbers to represent data and
    instructions. This makes it a little easier for a human to see what is going on. But other than
    that, it is a relatively accurate, if very simple, simulation of a computer. A real processor
    will have more registers to work with and will support dozens of instructions instead of the
    handful that the Little Computer does. But for the most part, everything they do is just as
    simple: they load/store data from memory, they do basic math and logic operations on data, and
    they use branches to make decisions and repeat operations. Whatever code a programmer writes in
    a <xref ref="programming-languages_high-level-programming-languages"
          text="custom">high-level
    language</xref> like javascript or C++ gets turned into these basic <xref ref="programming-languages_machine-languages"
          text="custom">machine
    language</xref> commands. </p>

  <p>
    This video introduces how the Little Computer works:
  </p>

  <video label="x7RMi6fA0jg"
         xml:id="x7RMi6fA0jg"
         youtube="x7RMi6fA0jg"
         width="100%"/>
  <p>
    You can run the Little Computer be run in a web page using the link below.
    The interface looks a little different than the video, but it functions the same way.
    It will launch with the program shown in the video.
    To try out a different program, simply edit the values that are in memory, and then press the
    reset button.
  </p>

  <p>
    <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/310/901/110/902/000"
         visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/310/901/110/902/000">Launch
      the Little Computer</url>
  </p>

  <note>
    <p> As you experiment with the little computer, notice that the memory is storing both the
      instructions and the data. An <q>instruction</q> is simply some data in memory that the
      computer tries to decode as a machine instruction and run. </p>
  </note>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-ex1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Why can the Little Computer use the same memory to store both instructions and ordinary
        data?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Both are values in memory; a value is treated as an instruction only when the processor
            decodes and executes it.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Instructions are stored in a separate kind of memory that data cannot enter.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Data automatically changes into an instruction after being stored.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>

      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The accumulator permanently holds every instruction in the program.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-ex2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Match each Little Computer component to its role.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise>Program counter</premise>
        <response>Keeps track of the next instruction to execute</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>Instruction register</premise>
        <response>Holds the instruction currently being decoded or executed</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>Accumulator</premise>
        <response>Holds a value used while the CPU performs operations</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-ex3">
    <statement>
      <p> Here is a simple program for the little computer. If you like, you can <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/312/901/112/312/112/902/000"
             visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/312/901/112/312/112/902/000">run
        it in
          the Little Computer simulator.</url>
      </p>
      <program>
        901
        312
        901
        112
        312
        112
        902
        000
      </program>
      <p>Match each machine instruction to a description of its role. Note that are some roles that
        do not have a corresponding instruction in this program.</p>
    </statement>
    <cardsort>
      <match>
        <premise>901</premise>
        <response>Get input from the user and place it in the accumulator</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <response>Get input from the user and store it in memory</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>312</premise>
        <response>Store the accumulator value into memory location 12</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <response>Store the value 12 into memory</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <response>Add 12 to the value in the accumulator</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>112</premise>
        <response>Add the value in memory location 12 to the value in the accumulator</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>902</premise>
        <response>Output the value in the accumulator to the user</response>
      </match>
      <match>
        <premise>000</premise>
        <response>Halt the program</response>
      </match>
    </cardsort>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-ex4">
    <statement>
      <p> Here is a simple program for the little computer. If you like, you can <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/312/901/112/312/112/902/000"
             visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html?program=901/312/901/112/312/112/902/000">run
        it in
          the Little Computer simulator.</url>
      </p>
      <program>
        901
        312
        901
        112
        312
        112
        902
        000
      </program>
      <p>Call the first value the user enters <c>x</c> and the second value <c>y</c>. What expression
        does the program compute?</p>
      <p>
        <fillin mode="math"
                ansobj="correctAnswer"/>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <setup>
      <de-object name="correctAnswer"
                 context="formula">
        <de-expression mode="formula">2*x+2*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>

    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
        </test>
        <test>
          <strcmp>*</strcmp>
          <feedback>
            <p> Make sure to write your answer as a math expression using the variables <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>
              .
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>

    <hint>
      <p>
        Try using various inputs to the program and see what output it produces. What do you get if
        you input 3 and 5? How about 10 and 20? If you carefully follow the steps of the program,
        you can figure out the general pattern of what it computes with the two inputs.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="programming-languages_the-little-computer-ex5">
    <statement>
      <p> Arrange the instructions to produce a program that computes 3y - x where x is the first
        input and y is the second input. You should use <url href="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html"
             visual="external/LittleComputer/littleComputer.html">
        the Little Computer simulator</url> to test your program. </p>
      <p>Note: there are multiple ways to arrange the instructions to achieve the desired result.
        And you could write a program that used different instructions to achieve the same result.</p>
    </statement>

    <blocks>
      <block order="4"
             name="a"
             depends="">
        <cline><![CDATA[901 (input x)]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="5"
             name="b"
             depends="a">
        <cline><![CDATA[315]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="6"
             name="c"
             depends="b">
        <cline><![CDATA[901 (input y)]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="8"
             name="d"
             depends="c">
        <cline><![CDATA[316]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="1"
             name="e"
             depends="d">
        <cline><![CDATA[116]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="7"
             name="f"
             depends="d">
        <cline><![CDATA[116]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="2"
             name="g"
             depends="d">
        <cline><![CDATA[215]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="9"
             name="h"
             depends="e f g">
        <cline><![CDATA[902]]></cline>
      </block>
      <block order="3"
             name="i"
             depends="h">
        <cline><![CDATA[000]]></cline>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </exercise>

</section>